## 3.5 Thermodynamic studies and reusability
The thermodynamic parameters including change in Gibbs free energy $(\Delta G^{0})$ , enthalpy $({\Delta H}^{0})$ and entropy $(\Delta S^{0})$ , can evaluate the spontaneity and feasibility of the adsorption process. Thermodynamic parameters were calculated using following equations [46-48]:
$$
\Delta G^{0}=\Delta H^{0}-T\Delta S^{0}
$$
$$
\ln k_{0}=\frac{\Delta S^{0}}{R}-\frac{\Delta H^{0}}{R T}
$$
$$
k_{\mathrm{0}}=\frac{q_{\mathrm{e}}}{C_{\mathrm{e}}}
$$
where $R$ (8.314 J/mol/K) and $k_{0}$ are the universal gas constant and the distribution constant, respectively. The ${\Delta H}^{0}$ and $\Delta S^{0}$ are calculated from the slope and intercept plot of $\mathrm{ln}k_{0}$ against $1/T$ (Fig. 7b) and optimized parameters are presented in Table S1. The values of $\Delta G^{0}$ decrease with the increase of temperature, indicating the higher temperatures is beneficial for $\mathrm{As(V)}$ removal. As shown in Fig. 7a, with the increasing temperature from 298 to $318\mathrm{~K~}$ , the removal amount of As(V) increases from 454.94 to $560.01~\mathrm{mg/g}$ , revealing the adsorption process is a chemical adsorption rather than physical adsorption [49]. The positive values of ${\Delta H}^{0}$ confirm the adsorption of As(V) is an endothermic process. The values of $\Delta S^{0}$ are also positive, indicating that the randomness increases at the solid/solution interface due to $\mathrm{As(V)}$ adhere to the surface of adsorbent. The negative $\Delta G^{0}$ values at all temperatures suggest that the removal is a spontaneous process.
The NiO/Ni@C400 shows a high saturation magnetization intensity around 30 emu/g (Fig. 7c). This larger magnetic behavior results from the high loading ratio of Ni nanoparticles in the carbon matrix. From the insert diagram from Fig. 7c, it is obvious that the NiO/Ni $@$ C400 was separated by a conventional magnet easily in 30 seconds, which is very convenient for the separation of adsorbent and adsorbate after removal of As(V) in industrials wastewater.
Considering the practicability of the adsorbent, regeneration and recyclability of adsorbent are very important. Therefore, the recycle performance of NiO/Ni $@$ C400 for As(V) removal was investigated. The desorption study of NiO/Ni@C400 was carried out. Firstly, the used adsorbent was washed with 1 mol/L NaOH solution, and the suspensions were separated under an external magnetic field after $24\mathrm{h}$ . After that, the recyclability was performed five times. As revealed in Fig. 7d, adsorption capacity of the NiO/Ni@C400 for As(V) decreases from 115 to $80~\mathrm{mg/g}$ after five times recycling, which still maintains a high removal performance. The result indicates that the NiO/Ni@C400 can be used as an efficient adsorbent to remove $\mathrm{As(V)}$ from wastewater.
