## 4. Conclusion
A fascinating magnetic chestnut shell-like hollow sphere structure composite was prepared by MOF as self-sacrificed precursor and used as the adsorbent for As(V) removal. Among the materials pyrolyzed at different temperatures, the NiO/Ni@C400 displayed a best adsorption capacity due to its abundant oxygen-containing functional groups and great specific surface area. Furthermore, the NiO/Ni@C400 adsorbent exhibited a very wide $\mathrm{pH}$ applicability from $\mathrm{pH}1$ to 10, with the maximum removal capacity was $454.94~\mathrm{mg/g}$ at $\mathrm{pH}3.0$ and 298 K. The adsorption process of $\operatorname{As}(\mathrm{V})$ was not easily affected by common coexisting ions. Additionally, adsorption kinetics experiments indicated that the removal of As(V) by NiO/Ni@C400 was a rapid and high-efficiency process. These findings confirm that NiO/Ni@C400 can be served as a promising adsorbent for treat As(V)-contaminated wastewater in environmental remediation applications.
